 "result_text": "`parse` now gives the right date for the issue's example. I changed the default for `dayfirst` from `True` to `False` in `delorean/interface.py`. With `dayfirst=True`, dateutil read the month and day of `2017-05-02` as 02 and 05. I also updated `docs/quickstart.rst`, which said the default was `dayfirst=True`.\n\nThe existing tests in `tests/delorean_tests.py` pass (89 passed). I did not add a test for this case.\n\nI couldn't run the issue's exact example here. In this environment `parse('2017-05-02T05:38:49.899Z')` raises `AttributeError: 'zoneinfo.ZoneInfo' object has no attribute 'localize'`. That comes from the `tzlocal` branch of `parse`, where the local timezone is now a `zoneinfo` object, and it isn't caused by this change. I didn't investigate it further. The earlier `dateutil` check showed that `dayfirst=False` gives `2017-05-02` for this string.\n\nThe new default changes how ambiguous numeric dates like `01/05/09` are read. They are now interpreted month-first unless the caller passes `dayfirst=True`.",
